 "findings": {
  "low_level": [
   "One bit pass over IPT items is IPT ballots of (candidate and bit) with their popcounts summed, no scan, no shuffle: the whole primitive is 35 registers at IPT 4 and 49 at IPT 8 with no spill and no local memory, occupancy 8 and 6 blocks per SM against W2_packed_v3's 64 registers at k 64 (occupancy 4).",
   "The pass count is data dependent through the exit when the candidate count equals the remaining k: on random fp32 the 32-bit walk stops after about as many passes as the 16-bit bf16 walk, both key types measure the same cycles within 3 percent in every cell (N 128 k 64: 1732 bf16 against 1739 fp32, N 256 k 128: 2369 against 2329), so the key width is not the cost driver on random data.",
   "The cost falls with k at N 256: 3216 cycles at k 16, 3423 at k 64, 2369 at k 128 (bf16), because the exit fires once the resolved prefix isolates exactly the remaining items, which happens after fewer bits when more items share the k-th key's neighbourhood. Latency fits R squared at least 0.996, the ballots pipeline across items (ptxas interleaves VOTE with the per-item predicate ALU)."
  ],
  "algorithmic": [
   "At warp scope N1 wins only where W2's per-lane list is deep: N 256 k 128 bf16 2369 against W2_packed_v3 5388 (2.3x) and fp32 2329 against 5063 (2.2x), ties at N 128 k 64 (1732 against 1775 bf16, 1739 against 1797 fp32), and loses 2 to 5x at k 16 (N 128: 2082 against 468, N 256: 3216 against 568) and at N 256 k 64 (3423 against 1868). N6w_v2 beats it in every shared cell (N 256 k 128 bf16 1661, k 64 1586, same node and day, different sessions).",
   "Throughput: 143 to 211 Gelem per s against W2's 85 to 190 at k 64 to 128 and 455 to 615 at k 16, so N1 is a throughput win only at k 128 and above at these N, where W2's lists of 128 words are the problem.",
   "The idea card's model (46 + 8 IPT per round, 16 rounds for bf16) predicted 1380 at N 128 k 64 and 1910 at N 256, measured 1732 and 3423: a round costs about 60 + 10 IPT (image shift, range test, ballot, popc and add per item, then a dependent tail of about 30 cycles for the sum, compare and range update), and the early exit, not the key width, sets the round count."
  ],
  "negative": [
   "N1 has no warp cell of its own on the focus grid: W2_packed_v3 holds k at most 64 at N 128 to 256 and N6w holds k 128. Its value is the d = 1 row of the digit sweep and the sub-warp form (ballots serve every logical warp at once, T37 and T40), which was not built."
  ],
  "surprises": [
   "fp32 costs the same as bf16 on random data because the exit on an exact candidate count fires after about the same number of bits for both: the walk stops when the prefix isolates exactly r keys, long before the 32 bits are consumed."
  ],
  "open_questions": [
   "The sub-warp form (one ballot per item serving 2 to 8 logical warps, per-row popcounts over byte masks) at LW 8 N 128 k 64, the idea card's own cell against W2_packed_sw_v2 (5261 cycles), is unmeasured.",
   "Whether a 2-bit digit per round (three popcounts, no scan) halves the round count at the same per-round cost."
  ]
 },
